  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nA slight cool down and increased rain chances are ahead at the start \nof the Long Term forecast. An upper trough begins to sag down across \nthe eastern half of the US Saturday, shoving the ridge that has kept \nus hot and dry further to our west. The trough ushers in a cold \nfront Saturday morning, offering some relief from the heat as highs \ntop out mainly in the 90s Saturday and Sunday. An added bonus with \nthis front is an upward tick in our rain chances. Some convection \nfrom Friday night may still be ongoing Saturday morning across the \nUpper Trans Pecos and SE NM. Saturday afternoon, rain chances across \nthe area range between 10-50%, with the best odds generally over SE \nNM. Saturday night into early Sunday afternoon, low (<20%) rain \nchances shift toward our southern counties. \n\nThe cool down is short-lived as upper ridging regains dominance over \nour weather pattern early next week. Triple digit heat sets back up \nwithin much of the Permian Basin, SE NM, and Upper Trans Pecos \nMonday afternoon. The hottest conditions will tend to lie near and \nalong the Rio Grande, especially within the Big Bend. Models suggest\nthe ridge expands east-west across the US and becomes flattened \nby a trough to its north Wednesday. As a result of lower heights, \ntemperatures midweek look to mainly reach the upper 90s. Overnight\nlows through the period generally cool into the 60s in/near the \nhigher terrain, with 70s at most other locations.",
